This version keeps the smallest possible ending value for every subsequence length. The `tailIndex` array stores indexes from the input, while `previous` stores links for rebuilding the answer.

When a number is not large enough to extend the current result, binary search finds the first tail greater than or equal to it. Replacing that tail keeps the same length but gives future numbers a better chance to extend it.

For example, the tail values for `[10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7]` become `[2, 3, 7]`. The links still point to real positions in the input, so the returned list is an actual subsequence.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> longestIncreasingSubsequence(int[] arr) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = arr.length;

        if (n == 0) {
            return answer;
        }

        int[] tailIndex = new int[n];
        int[] previous = new int[n];
        int length = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int left = 0;
            int right = length;

            while (left < right) {
                int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
                if (arr[tailIndex[middle]] < arr[i]) {
                    left = middle + 1;
                } else {
                    right = middle;
                }
            }

            previous[i] = left > 0 ? tailIndex[left - 1] : -1;
            tailIndex[left] = i;

            if (left == length) {
                length++;
            }
        }

        int index = tailIndex[length - 1];
        while (index != -1) {
            answer.add(arr[index]);
            index = previous[index];
        }

        java.util.Collections.reverse(answer);
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because each element uses one binary search.

Space complexity: `O(n)`. The input array is not changed.

I used the usual `dp[i]` idea, but also saved where each element came from. `dp[i]` is the longest increasing subsequence ending at `arr[i]`, and `previous[i]` lets us rebuild that sequence at the end.

For `[10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7]`, the best ending index points back through `7 -> 3 -> 2`. Reversing those values gives the answer in the original order.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    vector<int> longestIncreasingSubsequence(vector<int>& arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        if (n == 0) {
            return {};
        }

        vector<int> dp(n, 1);
        vector<int> previous(n, -1);
        int bestIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[i] && dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]) {
                    dp[i] = dp[j] + 1;
                    previous[i] = j;
                }
            }

            if (dp[i] > dp[bestIndex]) {
                bestIndex = i;
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        while (bestIndex != -1) {
            answer.push_back(arr[bestIndex]);
            bestIndex = previous[bestIndex];
        }

        reverse(answer.begin(), answer.end());
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the two arrays and the returned sequence.

I kept a direct recursive version here. For every element, the search tries both choices: leave it out, or include it if it is greater than the last selected element. It returns whichever vector is longer.

This is easy to compare with the problem statement, but it is slow because many choices are repeated. It is mostly useful for practicing the recurrence or for small arrays.

If all values are equal, every valid answer has one element. If the input is empty, the first base case returns an empty vector.

```cpp
class Solution {
  private:
    vector<int> solve(vector<int>& arr, int index, int previousIndex) {
        if (index == arr.size()) {
            return {};
        }

        vector<int> skip = solve(arr, index + 1, previousIndex);
        vector<int> take;

        if (previousIndex == -1 || arr[index] > arr[previousIndex]) {
            take = solve(arr, index + 1, index);
            take.insert(take.begin(), arr[index]);
        }

        return take.size() > skip.size() ? take : skip;
    }

  public:
    vector<int> longestIncreasingSubsequence(vector<int>& arr) {
        return solve(arr, 0, -1);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` in the worst case, since the recursion explores take and skip branches.

Space complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because of recursion depth and temporary vectors.
